1
00:00:00,861 --> 00:00:09,522
Welcome to Poland's regions, part 1 of 1. First, I clear the chosen names for
a fresh regional lesson.

2
00:00:10,178 --> 00:00:21,138
We work through 16 of the Poland regions the map names, once each: where it
sits, one fact worth keeping, and a hook to remember it by.

3
00:00:21,850 --> 00:00:25,426
Let's bring Pomorskie into view.

4
00:00:29,754 --> 00:00:44,174
Pomorskie runs along the Baltic coast in Poland’s north, with Gdańsk as its
largest city, between Zachodniopomorskie in the west and Warmińsko-Mazurskie
in the east.

5
00:00:44,831 --> 00:00:58,264
Its name means “along the sea” and shares its root with the German Pommern
next door, and Gdańsk was a free city between the wars before its shipyard
gave Solidarity its name in August 1980.

6
00:00:58,922 --> 00:01:10,439
Remember the finger of the Hel peninsula reaching 35 kilometres into the
Baltic on a spit barely 300 metres wide, and the shipyard gate where the
strikes began.

7
00:01:11,118 --> 00:01:13,928
Let's bring Warmińsko-Mazurskie into view.

8
00:01:18,120 --> 00:01:31,553
Warmińsko-Mazurskie lies inland in Poland’s north-east, with Olsztyn as its
largest city, between Pomorskie on the Baltic coast to the west and Podlaskie
in the east.

9
00:01:32,212 --> 00:01:43,938
The name pairs two peoples, the Varmians and the Masurians, and this is the
land of a thousand lakes, the last corner of Poland where Old Prussian was
still spoken into the 1700s.

10
00:01:44,593 --> 00:01:56,110
Remember the two tribes in one hyphen, the lakes between them, and the Elbląg
canal that lifts boats onto rail cradles and carries them overland.

11
00:01:56,797 --> 00:01:59,432
Let's bring Zachodniopomorskie into view.

12
00:02:03,630 --> 00:02:12,291
Zachodniopomorskie fills Poland’s north-west, on the Baltic and hard against
Germany, with Szczecin as its largest city.

13
00:02:12,951 --> 00:02:23,655
Its name is the western half of “along the sea”, and Szczecin was German until
1945, a city that is older than the Polish state itself.

14
00:02:24,309 --> 00:02:32,970
Remember the western along-the-sea, and the island town of Świnoujście, the
only Polish town spread over more than forty islands.

15
00:02:33,659 --> 00:02:35,958
Let's bring Podlaskie into view.

16
00:02:40,268 --> 00:02:52,726
Podlaskie sits in Poland’s far north-east, with Białystok as its largest city,
between Mazowieckie in the centre and Warmińsko-Mazurskie to the north-west.

17
00:02:53,377 --> 00:03:03,652
The name probably means “under the forest”, and Białowieża here holds one of
the last primeval lowland forests in Europe and the largest herd of wild
European bison.

18
00:03:04,322 --> 00:03:15,108
Remember the forest under the name, and the wooden Tatar mosques from the
1700s, settled by cavalry soldiers who were given land for their service.

19
00:03:15,794 --> 00:03:18,905
Let's bring Kujawsko-Pomorskie into view.

20
00:03:22,922 --> 00:03:33,835
Kujawsko-Pomorskie lies in north-central Poland, inland behind the Baltic
coast, with Bydgoszcz as its largest city and five other regions meeting
around it.

21
00:03:34,497 --> 00:03:43,797
The name joins two landscapes, the bare windy ground of Kujawy and the
sea-coast Pomorze, and Toruń here is where Copernicus was born.

22
00:03:44,457 --> 00:03:57,808
Remember the region of two halves, and the salt graduation towers of
Ciechocinek, where brine drips through blackthorn twigs and the air itself has
been breathed as a cure since 1824.

23
00:03:58,522 --> 00:04:02,144
Let's bring Wielkopolskie into view.

24
00:04:06,327 --> 00:04:14,860
Wielkopolskie spreads across Poland’s west-central plains, with Poznań as its
largest city, and it touches seven other regions.

25
00:04:15,521 --> 00:04:28,014
Greater Poland is greater because it is older, the first Polish state around
Gniezno and Poznań in the 900s, and Gniezno became Poland’s first
archbishopric in the year 1000.

26
00:04:28,671 --> 00:04:39,422
Remember the older Poland, where every early king was crowned at Gniezno
before Kraków took over, and which gave Małopolska its name as the younger
land.

27
00:04:40,107 --> 00:04:42,150
Let's bring Lubuskie into view.

28
00:04:46,987 --> 00:04:56,449
Lubuskie lies in Poland’s far west along the Oder, with Zielona Góra as its
largest city and Germany beyond the river.

29
00:04:57,103 --> 00:05:09,131
The region was created in 1999 and named after Lubusz, a town that lies on the
German side of the Oder, so it carries the name of a place it does not
contain.

30
00:05:09,789 --> 00:05:19,379
Remember the most wooded region in Poland, whose unfinished German bunkers
around Międzyrzecz now shelter the largest bat colony in Europe.

31
00:05:20,055 --> 00:05:22,783
Let's bring Mazowieckie into view.

32
00:05:27,096 --> 00:05:37,928
Mazowieckie spreads across the centre-east of Poland, with Warsaw as its
largest city and the capital of the whole country, ringed by six other
regions.

33
00:05:38,580 --> 00:05:51,073
The name comes from the Mazovians, a Polish tribe, and Warsaw was only a
Mazovian town until the king moved the court there from Kraków in 1596.

34
00:05:51,726 --> 00:06:04,985
Remember the birthplace of Chopin at Żelazowa Wola, and the 85 per cent of
Warsaw deliberately destroyed in 1944, then rebuilt from paintings of the city
that no longer existed.

35
00:06:05,670 --> 00:06:08,944
Let's bring Łódzkie into view.

36
00:06:12,677 --> 00:06:20,688
Łódzkie lies in the centre of Poland, with the city of Łódź as its largest
city and six regions meeting around it.

37
00:06:21,338 --> 00:06:33,575
The name means “boat” and the city’s coat of arms shows one, though it stands
on no navigable river, and Piotrkowska Street runs straight for over four
kilometres.

38
00:06:34,229 --> 00:06:43,993
Remember the boat on the coat of arms, and the textile city laid out like
Manchester, which grew from 800 people to 300,000 in a single century.

39
00:06:44,683 --> 00:06:47,922
Let's bring Lubelskie into view.

40
00:06:51,466 --> 00:07:01,915
Lubelskie lies in Poland’s east, with Lublin as its largest city, between
Mazowieckie in the centre and Podlaskie in the north-east.

41
00:07:02,576 --> 00:07:11,783
The Union of Lublin was signed here in 1569, joining Poland and Lithuania into
one commonwealth that lasted two hundred years.

42
00:07:12,446 --> 00:07:21,525
Remember the ideal Renaissance town of Zamość, built complete in the 1580s and
still standing exactly as it was first drawn.

43
00:07:22,217 --> 00:07:25,886
Let's bring Dolnośląskie into view.

44
00:07:29,080 --> 00:07:38,844
Dolnośląskie fills Poland’s south-west, with Wrocław as its largest city,
between Lubuskie in the west and the Czech border to the south.

45
00:07:39,506 --> 00:07:56,433
Lower Silesia takes its name from the Ślęża mountain and the tribe that lived
below it, and Wrocław has been Polish, Bohemian, Austrian, Prussian and German
before returning in 1945.

46
00:07:57,086 --> 00:08:06,037
Remember the region with more castles than any other in Poland, and Wrocław’s
centenary hall, the first great reinforced concrete dome.

47
00:08:06,714 --> 00:08:10,545
Let's bring Świętokrzyskie into view.

48
00:08:13,411 --> 00:08:23,384
Świętokrzyskie lies in south-central Poland, with Kielce as its largest city
and the low Holy Cross mountains standing in the middle of it.

49
00:08:24,038 --> 00:08:35,381
It is named for the abbey on Łysa Góra that claimed a relic of the True Cross,
and its mountains are among the oldest in Europe, worn down to gentle ridges
long before the Alps existed.

50
00:08:36,036 --> 00:08:45,545
Remember the oldest mountains in Poland, low and worn smooth, and the flint
mines of Krzemionki that were worked 5,000 years ago.

51
00:08:46,231 --> 00:08:48,193
Let's bring Opolskie into view.

52
00:08:53,187 --> 00:09:04,147
Opolskie is the smallest region in Poland, lying in the south with Opole as
its largest city, wedged between Dolnośląskie in the west and Śląskie in the
east.

53
00:09:04,802 --> 00:09:18,061
Its name comes from Opole, which in Old Slavic meant a community of villages
holding land together, and the region has Poland’s largest German minority, so
the road signs here are bilingual.

54
00:09:18,725 --> 00:09:29,511
Remember the small region whose name means the group of villages, where the
signposts read in two languages and the villages keep winning the best-kept
village competitions.

55
00:09:30,190 --> 00:09:33,336
Let's bring Śląskie into view.

56
00:09:36,563 --> 00:09:46,246
Śląskie occupies Poland’s far south-west, around Katowice as its largest city,
on the border with Czechia and Slovakia.

57
00:09:46,898 --> 00:09:56,743
It is Silesia without a qualifier, the upper part where the coal lies, and it
is the most densely populated region in the country.

58
00:09:57,409 --> 00:10:04,909
Remember the crowded coal country, where whole districts were built for miners
and the rail network is the densest in Poland.

59
00:10:05,591 --> 00:10:09,260
Let's bring Podkarpackie into view.

60
00:10:11,898 --> 00:10:21,279
Podkarpackie fills Poland’s south-east under the Carpathians, with Rzeszów as
its largest city and Ukraine close along its eastern edge.

61
00:10:21,937 --> 00:10:30,726
The name means “under the Carpathians”, and the region is one of the poorest
in Poland as well as the most forested after Lubuskie.

62
00:10:31,381 --> 00:10:42,515
Remember the oil wells dug by hand at Bóbrka in 1854, older by five years than
the Drake well in Pennsylvania that began the American oil rush.

63
00:10:43,276 --> 00:10:46,086
Let's bring Małopolskie into view.

64
00:10:54,212 --> 00:11:09,909
Małopolskie fills Poland’s south, with Kraków as its largest city, between
Świętokrzyskie in the north and Śląskie in the west, with the Carpathian hills
along its southern edge.

65
00:11:10,569 --> 00:11:26,010
Lesser Poland means the younger land, added after Greater Poland, and Kraków
was the capital for five centuries, keeping the royal castle, the university
and the coronation cathedral after the court left.

66
00:11:26,673 --> 00:11:36,391
Remember the salt mine at Wieliczka, worked since the 1200s, with its chapels
and chandeliers carved entirely from rock salt.

67
00:11:45,911 --> 00:12:16,295
16 regions of Poland, one for each target of the map: Pomorskie,
Warmińsko-Mazurskie, Zachodniopomorskie, Podlaskie, Kujawsko-Pomorskie,
Wielkopolskie, Lubuskie, Mazowieckie, Łódzkie, Lubelskie, Dolnośląskie,
Świętokrzyskie, Opolskie, Śląskie, Podkarpackie and Małopolskie.

68
00:12:16,954 --> 00:12:26,254
Remember the shape of Poland from the inside: the regions nest around each
other, and every name on the map belongs somewhere in this one country.
